The nonlocal conservation laws for $(u,u_1)$ with
\begin{align*}
(u,u_1) &\rightarrow (u\,/\,u_1,(u_\tau)_{\tau}):
\end{align*}
hold everywhere except possibly at a shock, according to Rankine-Hugoniot conditions. This is notable in modeling vehicular traffic, where the nonlocal nature of the conservation law can capture the true essence of the problem. However, the nonlocal wave system does not possess a unique solution unless the initial data have no shocks. This is due to an infinite number of possible shock interactions. Consequently, the model can realistically simulate the complex interactions of vehicles on the road, but it does not restrict shock interactions.

